\title{Object construction and validity in energyRt}
\description{
The package states one rule for how its S4 objects come into being and what
they guarantee once they exist. The rule is the contract the rest of the
package is written against, and it has three parts.
}
\section{Constructors are the supported path}{

Every class has a public constructor -- \code{\link[=newCommodity]{newCommodity()}}, \code{\link[=newTechnology]{newTechnology()}},
\code{\link[=newStorage]{newStorage()}}, \code{\link[=newModel]{newModel()}} and so on. The constructors are the only route
through the construction checks: column names are validated against the
class, types are coerced, renamed columns are diagnosed, and conflicting
rows are detected. An object returned by a constructor is a complete,
documented object of its class, and \code{methods::validObject()} accepts it.
}

\section{Direct \code{new()} refuses slot values}{

\code{new("<class>", slot = value)} raises an error naming the constructor to use
instead. The passthrough \code{initialize()} methods these classes define would
otherwise discard the value in silence -- a technology asked for by name
came back named \code{""} -- and assigning the slot directly would skip every
check listed above. \code{new("<class>")} with no arguments remains supported and
returns the class prototype.

Two generated classes are deliberate exceptions and still assign named
arguments to slots, because \code{newParameter()} and the internal model-data
machinery are built on that behaviour: \code{parameter} and \code{variable}.
}

\section{Prototypes are drafts, not public objects}{

A prototype obtained from a bare \code{new("<class>")} is an internal starting
point. It is not required to satisfy \code{validObject()}, and one does not:
a prototype \code{scenario} carries empty \code{model}, \code{settings}, \code{modInp} and
\code{modOut} slots, because building valid empties for them costs time that
would be paid on every package load for an object no user asks for.
\code{\link[=newScenario]{newScenario()}} returns a valid scenario; \code{new("scenario")} is a draft that
the interpolation and solve pipelines fill in.

Everything a user is handed -- by a constructor, by \code{\link[=interpolate]{interpolate()}}, by a
solve -- is a valid object of its class. Validity of a bare prototype is not
promised, and code that needs the guarantee should construct through the
constructor rather than validate a prototype.
}

